  "articleBody": "Applying fresh paint to basement walls can brighten dark lower levels, but peeling paint usually points to underlying moisture migrating through concrete foundation walls. Painting over dampness or efflorescence only traps water and causes premature failure.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start\n- Resolve foundation seepage: Never coat masonry that feels wet or shows signs of active leaks. Address exterior grading and gutter runoff first.\n- Clean off efflorescence: White powdery mineral deposits on concrete must be scrubbed off completely using a stiff brush; paint cannot bond to these salts.\n- Check previous coatings: In homes built before 1978, old basement paint might contain lead. Renovations disturbing those surfaces require lead-safe practices to prevent hazardous dust.\n- Select the right coating: Standard interior latex will fail on damp concrete. Use masonry primers and vapor-permeable or moisture-tolerant formulas suited to below-grade walls.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nIf peeling paint covers large foundation areas, or if drywall repairs and texture matching are needed alongside painting, calling an experienced remodeling contractor is the smartest route. Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC provides reliable drywall repair and interior painting across Denver and Aurora. Call 303-505-0866 to arrange a consultation for your basement.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
